<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>During the preparation of this article, S.C. was partially supported by NSF RTG grant DMS-1502651. H.L. was supported by the Hertz Foundation and NSF GRFP under grant DGE-1656518. This work will be part of S.C.’s and H.L.’s Ph.D. theses.</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Samir Canning, Hannah Larson
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:srcannin@ucsd.edu">srcannin@ucsd.edu</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:hlarson@stanford.edu">hlarson@stanford.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Hurwitz stack parametrizing degree <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, genus <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> covers of <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the tautological ring of <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> and we show that all Chow classes, except possibly those supported on the locus of “factoring covers,” are tautological up to codimension roughly <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g/k</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.
The set-up developed here is also used in our subsequent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, wherein we prove new results about the structure of the Chow ring for <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2010 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>14C15, 14C17
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When studying intersection theory of moduli spaces, one often introduces certain natural or “tautological” classes coming from the universal family. In the words of Mumford <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Whenever a variety or topological space is defined by some universal property, one expects that by virtue of its defining property, it possesses certain cohomology classes called tautological classes.</span></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For example, in the case of the moduli space of curves <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, the tautological classes Mumford proposes to study are the <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">kappa classes</em>, defined as follows. Let <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal curve; then <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{i}:=f_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{f})^{i+1})\in A^{i}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{i}:=f_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{f})^{i+1})\in A^{i}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, the Chow ring of <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tautological ring</em>, denoted <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})\subseteq A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})\subseteq A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, is the subring of the rational Chow ring generated by the kappa classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we study the intersection theory of the Hurwitz space <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, the moduli space of degree <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, genus <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> covers of <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, up to automorphisms of the target. Following Mumford’s philosophy, let us begin by introducing a notion of tautological classes. Let <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal curve and <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> the universal <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-fibration over the Hurwitz space <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="68.03" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-34.02px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 96.94 68.03" width="96.94"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,68.03) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(48.47,0) translate(0,34.02)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -48.47 -24.99)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 68.03)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.52)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(10.95,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -5 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.72em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="9.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.p4.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{C}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 32.15 0) translate(43.88,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -4.71 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.68em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="9.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.p4.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 59.01)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 21.91 0) translate(54.12,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -14.95 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.16em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.41" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="29.91"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.p4.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{H}_{k,g}.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{H}_{k,g}.}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -26.28 22.96 L 16.06 22.96"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 16.34 22.96)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -8.43 26.21)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.52em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="7.19"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.p4.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -26.28 15.28 L 10.52 -9.88"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.82549 -0.56442 0.56442 0.82549 10.75 -10.04)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -17.39 -7.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.p4.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 27.56 14.16 L 27.56 -9.64"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 27.56 -9.92)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 30.81 -0.11)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.47"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.p4.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\pi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We define the tautological subring of the Hurwitz space <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})\subseteq A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})\subseteq A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})</annotation></semantics></math> to be the subring generated by classes of the form <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{f})^{i}\cdot\alpha^{*}c_{1}(\omega_{\pi})^{j})=\pi_{*}(\alpha_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{f})^{i})\cdot c_{1}(\omega_{\pi})^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{f})^{i}\cdot\alpha^{*}c_{1}(\omega_{\pi})^{j})=\pi_{*}(\alpha_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{f})^{i})\cdot c_{1}(\omega_{\pi})^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In general, determining the full Chow ring of a moduli space — such as <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> — may be quite difficult. Having established a notion of tautological classes, however, it makes sense to split the study of the intersection theory of a moduli space into two parts:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 1:</span> To what extent are classes tautological? If they exist, what can we say about the support of non-tautological classes?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 2:</span> What is the structure of the tautological ring? Although the full Chow ring may be complicated, one hopes that the tautological ring has a more easily described structure.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we provide an answer to Question 1 for <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>. The ground work we develop here will also be important for addressing Question 2, which we undertake in subsequent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Before stating our results, we highlight some known results about the Chow ring of <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> related to Question 1 for context.</p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1a)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(codimension 1) Codimension 1 classes are tautological: <math id="S1.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{g})=R^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{g})=R^{1}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1b)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(low genus) For <math id="S1.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math>, all classes are tautological: <math id="S1.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1c)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(bielliptics) In genus <math id="S1.I2.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math>, the fundamental class of the bielliptic locus <math id="S1.I2.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.I2.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> tautological: <math id="S1.I2.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[B_{12}]\notin R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{12})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[B_{12}]\notin R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{12})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon the results for Hurwitz spaces in this paper and its sequel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, we extend (1b) to prove <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})=R^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Meanwhile, for the Hurwitz space <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, the previously known results regarding tautological classes are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1a)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(codimension 1) Codimension 1 classes are tautological <math id="S1.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{1}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})=R^{1}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{1}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})=R^{1}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.I3.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and <math id="S1.I3.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;g-1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. The general case remains an open conjecture known as the <em id="S1.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Picard rank conjecture</em>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1b)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(low degree) For <math id="S1.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, all classes are tautological: <math id="S1.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})=R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})=R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})</annotation></semantics></math>. In the case <math id="S1.I3.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>, it is well-known that <math id="S1.I3.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{2,g})=\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{2,g})=\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>; the case <math id="S1.I3.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> is due to Patel–Vakil <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Our main theorems make significant progress towards answering Question 1 for the next open cases: the Hurwitz spaces <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{5,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{5,g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A degree <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> cover <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can factor as two degree two covers <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to C^{\prime}\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to C^{\prime}\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g}^{\mathrm{nf}}\subset\mathcal{H}_{4,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>nf</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g}^{\mathrm{nf}}\subset\mathcal{H}_{4,g}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the open locus of <em id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-factoring covers</em>, or equivalently covers whose monodromy group is not contained in the dihedral group <math id="S1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. By <math id="S1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g}^{\mathrm{nf}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>nf</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g}^{\mathrm{nf}})</annotation></semantics></math> we mean the image of <math id="S1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})</annotation></semantics></math> under the restriction map <math id="S1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g}^{\mathrm{nf}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>nf</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g}^{\mathrm{nf}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If they exist, any non-tautological classes on <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,g}</annotation></semantics></math> are supported on the locus of factoring covers or have codimension at least <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g+3)/4-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g+3)/4-4</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{i}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g}^{\mathrm{nf}})=R^{i}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g}^{\mathrm{nf}})\quad\text{for all }i&lt;(g+3)/4-4." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>nf</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>nf</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{i}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g}^{\mathrm{nf}})=R^{i}(\mathcal{H}_{4,g}^{\mathrm{nf}})\quad\text{for all }i&lt;(g+3)/4-4.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fact that there may be non-tautological classes on the locus of factoring covers should be compared with (1c). In fact, using van Zelm’s result that <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[B_{12}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[B_{12}]</annotation></semantics></math> is not tautological, we establish in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Remark 1.10]</cite> that <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{4,12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{4,12}</annotation></semantics></math> indeed possesses non-tautological classes supported on the factoring locus.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In degree <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, covers cannot factor, and we obtain the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If they exist, any non-tautological classes on <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{5,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{5,g}</annotation></semantics></math> have codimension at least <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g+4)/5-16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g+4)/5-16</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{i}(\mathcal{H}_{5,g})=R^{i}(\mathcal{H}_{5,g})\quad\text{for all }i&lt;(g+4)/5-16." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{i}(\mathcal{H}_{5,g})=R^{i}(\mathcal{H}_{5,g})\quad\text{for all }i&lt;(g+4)/5-16.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> are reminiscent of the Madsen–Weiss theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, which proves Mumford’s conjecture that the stable cohomology of <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial ring in the kappa classes. Edidin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Question 3.34]</cite> asked if the analogue of the Madsen–Weiss theorem holds in the Chow ring <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, but very little is known about this question. We view Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> as providing some evidence toward a positive answer to Edidin’s question.
Unlike the case of the stable cohomology of <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, we will show in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> that there are many interesting relations among the tautological classes on <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq k\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq k\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Sketch of the proof</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are three key ingredients to proving Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>. The set up we develop will also be essential for later results determining structure of the tautological ring in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. We shall therefore carry them out in the case <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> as well, which fits into the same framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(1) Useful generators:</span>
We first explain how structure theorems of Casnati–Ekedahl for finite covers give rise to a collection of classes on <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we term Casnati–Ekedahl (CE) classes. In Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 3.10. ‣ 3.4. Casnati–Ekedahl classes ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>, we show that all CE classes are tautological and that they generate the tautological ring.
An interesting consequence of this is that, for fixed <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim R^{i}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim R^{i}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded above, independent of <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. (This part works for any <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>; see Remark <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Remark 3.11. ‣ 3.4. Casnati–Ekedahl classes ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(2) The good open:</span> For <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3,4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3,4,5</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a “good open” <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}\subseteq\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}\subseteq\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using our interpretation of the Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorems, we show that this “good open” possesses an open embedding inside a vector bundle <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{k,g}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{k,g}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> over a moduli space <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{k,g}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{k,g}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of pairs of vector bundles on <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The pullbacks of classes along <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{B}_{k,g}^{\prime})=A^{*}(\mathcal{X}_{k,g}^{\prime})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{B}_{k,g}^{\prime})=A^{*}(\mathcal{X}_{k,g}^{\prime})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are CE classes (essentially by the definition of CE classes). It follows that <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by tautological classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SSx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(3) Codimension bounds:</span> By excision, there is a surjection <math id="S1.SSx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g})\to A^{*}(\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> whose kernel is generated by classes supported on the complement of <math id="S1.SSx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the final step is to bound the codimension of the complement of <math id="S1.SSx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S1.SSx1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math>, it turns out <math id="S1.SSx1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}=\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}=\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we recover the result of Patel–Vakil <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> that all classes are tautological. When <math id="S1.SSx1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4</annotation></semantics></math>, the complement of <math id="S1.SSx1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the locus of covers that factor through a double cover of a low-genus curve. Thus, the complement has codimension 2. However, it turns out that the <em id="S1.SSx1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-factoring</em> covers in the complement of <math id="S1.SSx1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> have codimension at least <math id="S1.SSx1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g+3)/4-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g+3)/4-4</annotation></semantics></math>. This leads to the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> at the end of Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Degree 
          
            4 ‣ 5. The good opens and codimension bounds ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.
Finally, for <math id="S1.SSx1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math>, there are no factoring covers, and we show that the complement of <math id="S1.SSx1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension at least <math id="S1.SSx1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g+4)/5-16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g+4)/5-16</annotation></semantics></math>. With this, we conclude the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> at the end of Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Degree 
          
            5 ‣ 5. The good opens and codimension bounds ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgments</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are grateful to our advisors, Elham Izadi and Ravi Vakil, respectively, for the many helpful conversations. We are grateful to Aaron Landesman for his comments and insights.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Notation and conventions</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will work over an algebraically closed field of characteristic <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or characteristic <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;5</annotation></semantics></math>. All schemes in this paper will be taken over this fixed field.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Projective bundles</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We follow the subspace convention for projective bundles: given a scheme (or stack) <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and a vector bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we set</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}E:=\Proj(\Sym^{\bullet}E^{\vee})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Proj</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}E:=\Proj(\Sym^{\bullet}E^{\vee}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">so we have the tautological inclusion</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E}(-1)\hookrightarrow\gamma^{*}E," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E}(-1)\hookrightarrow\gamma^{*}E,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma:\mathbb{P}E\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:\mathbb{P}E\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> is the structure map. Set <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta:=c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta:=c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E}(1))</annotation></semantics></math>. With this convention, the Chow ring of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}E</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathbb{P}E)=A^{*}(X)[\zeta]/\langle\zeta^{r}+\zeta^{r-1}c_{1}(E)+\ldots+c_{r}(E)\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathbb{P}E)=A^{*}(X)[\zeta]/\langle\zeta^{r}+\zeta^{r-1}c_{1}(E)+\ldots+c_{r}(E)\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">We call this the <em id="S2.SS1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">projective bundle theorem</em>.
Note that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\zeta,\zeta^{2},\ldots,\zeta^{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\zeta,\zeta^{2},\ldots,\zeta^{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> form a basis for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(\mathbb{P}E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(\mathbb{P}E)</annotation></semantics></math> as an <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Since</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{*}\zeta^{i}=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{if $i\leq r-2$}\\
1&amp;\text{if $i=r-1$,}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{*}\zeta^{i}=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{if $i\leq r-2$}\\
1&amp;\text{if $i=r-1$,}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">this determines the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of all classes from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}E</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>(Equivariant) Intersection Theory</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a scheme and suppose <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed subscheme of codimension <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is its open complement. We denote the Chow ring of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with rational coefficients by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">excision property</em> of Chow is the right exact sequence</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*-c}(Z)\rightarrow A^{*}(X)\rightarrow A^{*}(U)\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*-c}(Z)\rightarrow A^{*}(X)\rightarrow A^{*}(U)\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">If one knows <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, then to find the Chow ring of an open <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>, one must describe the image of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*-c}(Z)\rightarrow A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*-c}(Z)\rightarrow A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{Z}\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{Z}\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and surjective, then pushforward <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*}(\widetilde{Z})\to A_{*}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*}(\widetilde{Z})\to A_{*}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, Lemma 1.2]</cite>.
Given a graded ring <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\bigoplus R^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\bigoplus R^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\trun^{d}R:=R/\oplus_{i\geq d}R^{d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Trun</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mi>R</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trun^{d}R:=R/\oplus_{i\geq d}R^{d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">denote the degree <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> trunction. With this notation, if the complement of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, then the excision property implies</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trun^{c}A^{*}(X)\xrightarrow{\sim}\trun^{c}A^{*}(U)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Trun</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Trun</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trun^{c}A^{*}(X)\xrightarrow{\sim}\trun^{c}A^{*}(U).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Chow rings also satisfy the <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">homotopy property</em>: if <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle, then the pullback map <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)\to A^{*}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)\to A^{*}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. This property motivates the definition of equivariant Chow groups as developed by Edidin-Graham in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Again, we will be using rational coefficients for our equivariant Chow rings.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a representation of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset V</annotation></semantics></math> and the codimension of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\smallsetminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\smallsetminus U</annotation></semantics></math> is greater than <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth scheme and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear algebraic group acting on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, Edidin and Graham defined</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{c}_{G}(X):=A^{c}((X\times U)/G)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{c}_{G}(X):=A^{c}((X\times U)/G),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and showed that the graded ring <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> possesses an intersection product.
For quotient stacks, one has <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}([X/G])\cong A^{*}_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}([X/G])\cong A^{*}_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Proposition 19]</cite>, which may suffice as the definition of the Chow rings of all stacks appearing in this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By Edidin-Graham <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Proposition 5]</cite>, there is also an excision sequence for equivariant Chow groups. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subseteq X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant closed subscheme of codimension <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> its complement. Then there is an exact sequence</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*-c}_{G}(Z)\rightarrow A^{*}_{G}(X)\rightarrow A^{*}_{G}(U)\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*-c}_{G}(Z)\rightarrow A^{*}_{G}(X)\rightarrow A^{*}_{G}(U)\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The following lemma is a useful consequence of the excision sequence. See also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, Theorem 2]</cite> for a much more general statement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> is a principal <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(P)=A^{*}(X)/\langle c_{1}(L)\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(P)=A^{*}(X)/\langle c_{1}(L)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding line bundle.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the correspondence between principal <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles and line bundles over <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is the complement of the zero section of the line bundle <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math>. The excision sequence gives</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*-1}(X)\rightarrow A^{*}(L)\rightarrow A^{*}(P)\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*-1}(X)\rightarrow A^{*}(L)\rightarrow A^{*}(P)\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Under the identification of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, the first map in the above exact sequence is multiplication by <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, from which the result follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>The Hurwitz space</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a scheme <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, an <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> point of the <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parametrized Hurwitz scheme</em> <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> is the data of a finite, flat map <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}\times S</annotation></semantics></math>, of constant degree <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> so that the composition <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}\times S\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}\times S\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth with geometrically connected fibers. (We do not impose the condition that a cover <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be simply branched.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unparametrized Hurwitz stack</em> is the <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PGL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PGL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> quotient of the parametrized Hurwitz scheme. There is also a natural action of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> (via <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}\subset\GL_{2}\to\PGL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>GL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}\subset\GL_{2}\to\PGL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). The natural map <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\dagger}/\SL_{2}]\to[\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\dagger}/\PGL_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\dagger}/\SL_{2}]\to[\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\dagger}/\PGL_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> banded gerbe. It is a general fact that <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">with rational coefficients</em>, the pullback map along a gerbe banded by a finite group is an isomorphism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, Section 2.3]</cite>. In particular, since we work with rational coefficients throughout, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}([\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\dagger}/\PGL_{2}])\cong A^{*}([\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{k,g}/\SL_{2}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}([\mathcal{H}_{k,g}^{\dagger}/\PGL_{2}])\cong A^{*}([\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{k,g}/\SL_{2}])</annotation></semantics></math>. It thus suffices to prove all statements for the <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> quotient <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{k,g}/\SL_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{k,g}/\SL_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which we shall denote by <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> from now on.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Explicitly, the <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> quotient <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stack whose objects over a scheme <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are families <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C\rightarrow P\rightarrow S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C\rightarrow P\rightarrow S)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\mathbb{P}V\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\mathbb{P}V\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math> is the projectivization of a rank <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> with trivial determinant, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow P</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite, flat, finitely presented morphism of constant degree <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and the composition <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math> has smooth fibers of genus <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
The benefit of working with <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is that the <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> quotient is equipped with a universal <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{H}_{k,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{H}_{k,g}</annotation></semantics></math> that has a relative degree one line bundle <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{P}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{P}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> (a <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-fibration does not).
Working with this <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle simplifies our intersection theory calculations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main objective of this section is to give a description of stacks of low-degree covers using structure theorems of Casnati–Ekedahl. The descriptions in Sections <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. The category of triple covers ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>–<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3. The category of regular pentagonal covers ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> are likely well-known but have not previously been spelled out in the language of stacks except in the degree 3 case <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, as we shall need them. On a first pass, the reader may wish to skip forward to Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Casnati–Ekedahl classes ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, where we introduce natural classes coming from these structure theorems and prove that they generate the tautological ring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Generalizing earlier results of Schreyer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> and Miranda <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, Casnati–Ekedahl <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> proved a general structure theorem for degree <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, Gorenstein covers of integral schemes. Given a degree <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> cover <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is integral, one obtains an exact sequence</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{Y}\rightarrow\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow E_{\alpha}^{\vee}\rightarrow 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{Y}\rightarrow\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow E_{\alpha}^{\vee}\rightarrow 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle of rank <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is Gorenstein, <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X}\cong(\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X}\cong(\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> by Serre duality.
Pulling back and using adjunction, we therefore obtain a map</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\alpha}^{\vee}\to(\alpha^{*}\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha})^{\vee}\rightarrow\alpha^{*}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\alpha}^{\vee}\to(\alpha^{*}\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha})^{\vee}\rightarrow\alpha^{*}E_{\alpha}^{\vee},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which induces a map <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\rightarrow\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\rightarrow\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> that factors <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Covers of <math id="S3.Thmthm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S3.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a degree <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, genus <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> cover, then we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(E_{\alpha}^{\vee})=\deg(\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{C})=\chi(\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{C})-k=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{C})-k=1-g-k," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(E_{\alpha}^{\vee})=\deg(\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{C})=\chi(\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{C})-k=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{C})-k=1-g-k,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">so <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(E_{\alpha})=g+k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(E_{\alpha})=g+k-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The map <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> factors the canonical embedding <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}^{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}^{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the map <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}_{\alpha}\to\mathbb{P}^{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}_{\alpha}\to\mathbb{P}^{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the line bundle <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}}(1)\otimes\omega_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}}(1)\otimes\omega_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each linear space in the image of <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}\to\mathbb{P}^{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}\to\mathbb{P}^{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the span of the image of the corresponding fiber of <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem below gives a resolution of the ideal sheaf of <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> inside of <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>; see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Casnati–Ekedahl, Theorem 2.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be schemes, <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> integral and let <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a Gorenstein cover of degree <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. There exists a unique <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{k-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{k-2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma:\mathbb{P}\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:\mathbb{P}\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> and an embedding <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:X\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:X\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\gamma\circ i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\gamma\circ i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}:=\alpha^{-1}(y)\subset\gamma^{-1}(y)\cong\mathbb{P}^{k-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}:=\alpha^{-1}(y)\subset\gamma^{-1}(y)\cong\mathbb{P}^{k-2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nondegenerate arithmetically Gorenstein subscheme for each <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the following properties hold.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\cong\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\cong\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> where </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\vee}_{\alpha}:=\coker(\mathcal{O}_{Y}\rightarrow\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\vee}_{\alpha}:=\coker(\mathcal{O}_{Y}\rightarrow\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The composition </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}E_{\alpha}\to\alpha^{*}\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}\to\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}E_{\alpha}\to\alpha^{*}\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}\to\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is surjective (dually, (</span><a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) does not drop rank) and the ramification divisor </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfies </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}(R)\cong\omega_{\alpha}\cong\mathcal{O}_{X}(1):=i^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}(R)\cong\omega_{\alpha}\cong\mathcal{O}_{X}(1):=i^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists an exact sequence of locally free </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> sheaves</span></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F_{k-2}(-k)\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F_{k-3}(-k+2)\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F_{1}(-2)\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F_{k-2}(-k)\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F_{k-3}(-k+2)\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F_{1}(-2)\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is locally free on </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
The restriction of the exact sequence above to a fiber gives a minimal free resolution of </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}:=\alpha^{-1}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}:=\alpha^{-1}(y)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. This sequence is unique up to unique isomorphism. Moreover the resolution is self-dual, meaning there is a canonical isomorphism </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}om_{\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}}(F_{i},F_{k-2})\cong F_{k-2-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}om_{\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}}(F_{i},F_{k-2})\cong F_{k-2-i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. The ranks of the </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rank F_{i}=\frac{i(k-2-i)}{k-1}{{k}\choose{i+1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rank F_{i}=\frac{i(k-2-i)}{k-1}{{k}\choose{i+1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\cong\mathbb{P}E^{\prime\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>′</mo><mo lspace="0.222em">⁣</mo><mo>∨</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\cong\mathbb{P}E^{\prime\vee}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}\cong E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}\cong E</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if and only if </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k-2}\cong\det E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k-2}\cong\det E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in the resolution (</span><a href="#S3.E3" title="In item 3 ‣ Theorem 3.2 (Casnati–Ekedahl, Theorem 2.1 of []). ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) computed with respect to the polarization </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\prime\vee}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>′</mo><mo lspace="0.222em">⁣</mo><mo>∨</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\prime\vee}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a canonical isomorphism <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k-2}\cong\det E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k-2}\cong\det E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we describe here. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, p. 446]</cite>, let <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the image of <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}F_{1}(-2)\to\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}F_{1}(-2)\to\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
for <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq i\leq k-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq i\leq k-3</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the image of <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}F_{i}(-i-1)\to\gamma^{*}F_{i-1}(-i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}F_{i}(-i-1)\to\gamma^{*}F_{i-1}(-i)</annotation></semantics></math>. We set <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k-2}</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}F_{k-2}(-k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}F_{k-2}(-k)</annotation></semantics></math>. We have exact sequences</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow A_{1}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow A_{1}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow A_{i+1}\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F_{i}(-i-1)\rightarrow A_{i}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow A_{i+1}\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F_{i}(-i-1)\rightarrow A_{i}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">First, we claim that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{j}\gamma_{*}\gamma^{*}F_{i}(-i-1)\cong\begin{cases}F_{k-2}\otimes\det E^{\vee}&amp;\text{if $i=j=k-2$}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{j}\gamma_{*}\gamma^{*}F_{i}(-i-1)\cong\begin{cases}F_{k-2}\otimes\det E^{\vee}&amp;\text{if $i=j=k-2$}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">This is very similar to the calculations of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, p. 446]</cite>, but twisted up by one.
To prove the first case above, we note that the dualizing sheaf of <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\gamma}=(\gamma^{*}\det E)(-k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\gamma}=(\gamma^{*}\det E)(-k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and apply Serre duality for <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, which is of relative dimension <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The other cases follow from the theorem on cohomology and base change and the well-known cohomology of line bundles on projective space.
Tensoring the exact sequences of (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In Remark 3.3. ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>) by <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and pushing forward by <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, the boundary maps provide us with isomorphisms</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{*}A_{1}(1)\cong R^{1}\gamma_{*}A_{2}(1)\cong R^{2}\gamma_{*}A_{3}(1)\cong\cdots\cong R^{k-1}\gamma_{*}(\gamma^{*}F_{k-2}(-k+1))=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{*}A_{1}(1)\cong R^{1}\gamma_{*}A_{2}(1)\cong R^{2}\gamma_{*}A_{3}(1)\cong\cdots\cong R^{k-1}\gamma_{*}(\gamma^{*}F_{k-2}(-k+1))=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Similarly, we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}\gamma_{*}A_{1}(1)\cong R^{2}\gamma_{*}A_{2}(1)\cong\cdots\cong R^{k-2}\gamma_{*}(\gamma^{*}F_{k-2}(-k+1))\cong F_{k-2}\otimes\det E^{\vee}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}\gamma_{*}A_{1}(1)\cong R^{2}\gamma_{*}A_{2}(1)\cong\cdots\cong R^{k-2}\gamma_{*}(\gamma^{*}F_{k-2}(-k+1))\cong F_{k-2}\otimes\det E^{\vee}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.6" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, tensoring (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In Remark 3.3. ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>) with <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and pushing forward by <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow E\rightarrow\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X}(1)\rightarrow R^{1}\gamma_{*}A_{1}(1)\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow E\rightarrow\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X}(1)\rightarrow R^{1}\gamma_{*}A_{1}(1)\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.7" class="ltx_p">Recall that <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}(1)\cong\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}(1)\cong\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, so
dualizing (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) we see that the cokernel of the left map is <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the universal property of cokernel, we obtain an isomorphism</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y}\to R^{1}\gamma_{*}A_{1}(1)\cong F_{k-2}\otimes\det E^{\vee}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y}\to R^{1}\gamma_{*}A_{1}(1)\cong F_{k-2}\otimes\det E^{\vee},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.8" class="ltx_p">or equivalently, an isomorphism <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k-2}\cong\det E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k-2}\cong\det E</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the cases <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3,4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3,4,5</annotation></semantics></math>, using self-duality, only pullbacks of the bundles <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and determinants and tensor products thereof appear in the resolution (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In item 3 ‣ Theorem 3.2 (Casnati–Ekedahl, Theorem 2.1 of []). ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).
We set <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\alpha}:=F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\alpha}:=F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Twisting up (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In item 3 ‣ Theorem 3.2 (Casnati–Ekedahl, Theorem 2.1 of []). ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) by <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}}(2)</annotation></semantics></math> and pushing forward by <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\alpha}=\ker(\Sym^{2}E_{\alpha}\twoheadrightarrow\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes 2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\alpha}=\ker(\Sym^{2}E_{\alpha}\twoheadrightarrow\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes 2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">In these low degrees <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3,4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3,4,5</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a special map <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> in the resolution (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In item 3 ‣ Theorem 3.2 (Casnati–Ekedahl, Theorem 2.1 of []). ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) from which one can reconstruct the cover. Furthermore, as we shall explain, it is an <em id="S3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">open condition</em> on a space of global sections of all such maps <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> to define a finite cover. This is what distinguishes <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3,4,5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3,4,5</annotation></semantics></math> and lies at the core of why our methods work in these low degrees. Below we present an equivalence of categories between the category of degree <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, Gorenstein covers of a scheme <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and a category of certain linear algebraic data on <math id="S3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. The main content of this step is to point out the “essential data” of a cover, which we may remember instead of the entire resolution. For the case of triple covers, this was done by Bolognesi–Vistoli <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. We give a slightly different perspective below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>The category of triple covers</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Trip}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Trip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Trip}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the category of Gorenstein triple covers of a scheme <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>: the objects are Gorenstein triple covers <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> and the arrows are isomorphisms over <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specializing (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In item 3 ‣ Theorem 3.2 (Casnati–Ekedahl, Theorem 2.1 of []). ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) to the case <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math>, associated to a cover <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math>, one obtains a rank <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and an exact sequence</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}}(-3)\otimes\gamma^{*}\det E_{\alpha}\xrightarrow{\delta_{\alpha}}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">α</mi></msub></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}}(-3)\otimes\gamma^{*}\det E_{\alpha}\xrightarrow{\delta_{\alpha}}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Conversely, from the above sequence, we can recover the cover <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:X\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:X\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, the map <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a global section in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee},\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}_{\alpha}}(3)\otimes\gamma^{*}\det E_{\alpha}^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee},\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}_{\alpha}}(3)\otimes\gamma^{*}\det E_{\alpha}^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math>, whose zero locus inside of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Meanwhile, given any rank <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, it is an open condition on the space of sections
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(3)\otimes\gamma^{*}\det E^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(3)\otimes\gamma^{*}\det E^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math>
for the vanishing of a section <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> to define a finite triple cover: <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> must not be the zero polynomial on any fiber of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}E\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}E\to S</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, if</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi:H^{0}(S,\Sym^{3}E\otimes\det E^{\vee})\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(3)\otimes\gamma^{*}\det E^{\vee})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:H^{0}(S,\Sym^{3}E\otimes\det E^{\vee})\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(3)\otimes\gamma^{*}\det E^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">denotes the natural isomorphism, then <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(\delta)\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\delta)\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Gorenstein triple cover so long as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{-1}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{-1}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-vanishing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This “essential data” is captured by a category <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Trip}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Trip</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Trip}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> we now define. The objects of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Trip}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Trip</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Trip}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> are pairs <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is a rank <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in H^{0}(S,\Sym^{3}E\otimes\det E^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in H^{0}(S,\Sym^{3}E\otimes\det E^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math> is non-vanshing on <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
An arrow <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E_{1},\eta_{1})\to(E_{2},\eta_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E_{1},\eta_{1})\to(E_{2},\eta_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Trip}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Trip</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Trip}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}\to E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}\to E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> that sends <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
There is a functor <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Trip}(S)\to\mathrm{Trip}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Trip</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Trip</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Trip}(S)\to\mathrm{Trip}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> that sends <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> to the pair <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E_{\alpha},\Phi^{-1}(\delta_{\alpha}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E_{\alpha},\Phi^{-1}(\delta_{\alpha}))</annotation></semantics></math>. There is also a functor <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Trip}^{\prime}(S)\to\mathrm{Trip}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Trip</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Trip</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Trip}^{\prime}(S)\to\mathrm{Trip}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> that sends a pair <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> to the triple cover <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(\Phi(\eta))\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\Phi(\eta))\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}\to S</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following is essentially a restatement of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Theorem 3.4]</cite>, which was proved earlier by Miranda <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Miranda, Casnati–Ekedahl)<span id="S3.Thmthm4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The functors above define an equivalence of categories <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Trip}(S)\cong\mathrm{Trip}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Trip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Trip</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Trip}(S)\cong\mathrm{Trip}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>The category of quadruple covers</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Quad}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Quad</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Quad}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the category whose objects are Gorenstein quadruple covers <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> and whose arrows are isomorphisms over <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Associated to a degree <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> cover <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a rank <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and a rank <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and a resolution</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow\gamma^{*}\det E_{\alpha}(-4)\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F_{\alpha}(-2)\xrightarrow{\delta_{\alpha}}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">α</mi></msub></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\gamma^{*}\det E_{\alpha}(-4)\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F_{\alpha}(-2)\xrightarrow{\delta_{\alpha}}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The section <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\alpha}\in H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee},\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}}(2)\otimes\gamma^{*}F^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\alpha}\in H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee},\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E_{\alpha}^{\vee}}(2)\otimes\gamma^{*}F^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a relative pencil of quadrics. The cover
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> can be recovered as the vanishing locus of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. By comparing (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.2. The category of quadruple covers ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>) with the Koszul resolution of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow\gamma^{*}\det F_{\alpha}(-4)\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F_{\alpha}(-2)\xrightarrow{\delta_{\alpha}}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}_{\alpha}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">α</mi></msub></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\gamma^{*}\det F_{\alpha}(-4)\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F_{\alpha}(-2)\xrightarrow{\delta_{\alpha}}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}_{\alpha}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">the uniqueness of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Casnati–Ekedahl, Theorem 2.1 of []). ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> (3) induces a distinguished isomorphism <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\alpha}:\det F_{\alpha}\cong\det E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.111em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\alpha}:\det F_{\alpha}\cong\det E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, p. 450]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now define a category <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Quad</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> of the corresponding linear algebraic data of a quadruple cover. Given vector bundles <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E,F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E,F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>,
there is a natural isomorphism</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:H^{0}(S,F^{\vee}\otimes\Sym^{2}E)\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},\gamma^{*}F^{\vee}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:H^{0}(S,F^{\vee}\otimes\Sym^{2}E)\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{0}(\mathbb{P}E^{\vee},\gamma^{*}F^{\vee}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(2)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be vector bundles of ranks <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> respectively on <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that a section <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in H^{0}(S,F^{\vee}\otimes\Sym^{2}E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in H^{0}(S,F^{\vee}\otimes\Sym^{2}E)</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">has the right codimension</em> at <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> if the vanishing locus of <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> restricted to the fiber over <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> is zero dimensional.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The objects of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Quad</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> are tuples <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E,F,\phi,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E,F,\phi,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> are vector bundles of ranks <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\det F\cong\det E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.111em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\det F\cong\det E</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in H^{0}(S,F^{\vee}\otimes\Sym^{2}E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in H^{0}(S,F^{\vee}\otimes\Sym^{2}E)</annotation></semantics></math> has the right codimension at all <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. An arrow in <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Quad</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> is a pair of isomorphisms <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi:E_{1}\to E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi:E_{1}\to E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:F_{1}\to F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:F_{1}\to F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the following diagrams commute</p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="73.07" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-36.54px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 139.56 73.07" width="139.56"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,73.07) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(63.1,0) translate(0,36.54)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -63.1 -28.78)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 73.07)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.33)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(14.47,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -8.51 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.23em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="17.03"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 28.94 0) translate(65.24,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -26.07 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.77em;--ltx-fo-height:0.81em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.95" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.26)" width="52.14"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Sym^{2}E_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Sym^{2}E_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 65.32)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(14.47,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -8.51 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.23em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="17.03"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 28.94 0) translate(65.24,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -26.07 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.77em;--ltx-fo-height:0.81em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.95" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.26)" width="52.14"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Sym^{2}E_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Sym^{2}E_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -48.63 12.79 L -48.63 -13.43"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -48.63 -13.71)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -59.21 -3.02)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.53em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="7.32"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\psi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -33.88 23.67 L -1.78 23.67"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -1.5 23.67)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -23.11 28.81)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.8em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="11.1"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\eta_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">η</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\eta_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 31.08 12.18 L 31.08 -11.62"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 31.08 -11.9)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 34.33 -3.39)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.83em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.48)" width="39.15"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Sym^{2}\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Sym^{2}\xi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -33.88 -25.32 L -1.78 -25.32"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -1.5 -25.32)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -23.11 -20.18)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.8em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="11.1"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\eta_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">η</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\eta_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p3.pic2" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="72.16" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-34.27px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 145.01 72.16" width="145.01"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,72.16) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(74.66,0) translate(0,34.27)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -69.09 -27.13)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 68.53)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.67)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(25.23,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -19.28 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="38.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\det F_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\det F_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 52.39 0) translate(58.54,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -19.37 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.8em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="38.74"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\det E_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\det E_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 61.39)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(25.23,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -19.28 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="38.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\det F_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\det F_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 50.47 0) translate(60.46,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -21.29 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.08em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="42.59"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\det E_{2}.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\det E_{2}.}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -18.35 23.05 L 15.68 23.05"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.95 23.05)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -7.08 28.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.87em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="12.05"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\phi_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\phi_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -43.86 12.18 L -43.86 -11.62"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -43.86 -11.9)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -71.68 -2.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="24.57"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\det\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\det\psi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 41.84 12.18 L 41.84 -11.62"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 41.84 -11.9)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 45.09 -2.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.61em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="22.28"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\det\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\det\xi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -18.35 -23.67 L 13.76 -23.67"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 14.03 -23.67)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -8.04 -18.53)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.87em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="12.05"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.pic2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\phi_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\phi_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">There is a functor <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Quad}(S)\to\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Quad</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Quad</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Quad}(S)\to\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> that sends <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E_{\alpha},F_{\alpha},\phi_{\alpha},\eta_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E_{\alpha},F_{\alpha},\phi_{\alpha},\eta_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\alpha}:=\Phi^{-1}(\delta_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\alpha}:=\Phi^{-1}(\delta_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>.
There is also a functor <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)\to\mathrm{Quad}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Quad</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Quad</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)\to\mathrm{Quad}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> that sends a tuple <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E,F,\phi,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E,F,\phi,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> to the quadruple cover <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(\Phi(\eta))\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\Phi(\eta))\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}\to S</annotation></semantics></math>. The following is essentially a restatement of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Theorem 4.4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Casnati–Ekedahl)<span id="S3.Thmthm6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The functors above define an equivalence of categories <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Quad}(S)\cong\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Quad</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Quad</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Quad}(S)\cong\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Work of Casnati–Ekedahl established that the composition <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Quad}(S)\to\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)\to\mathrm{Quad}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Quad</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Quad</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Quad</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Quad}(S)\to\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)\to\mathrm{Quad}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the identity, as <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(\delta_{\alpha})\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\delta_{\alpha})\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally identified with the cover <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We must provide a natural isomorphism of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)\to\mathrm{Quad}(S)\to\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Quad</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Quad</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Quad</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)\to\mathrm{Quad}(S)\to\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> with the identity on <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Quad</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose we are given <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E,F,\phi,\eta)\in\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Quad</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E,F,\phi,\eta)\in\mathrm{Quad}^{\prime}(S)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We want to define an arrow <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E,F,\phi,\eta)\to(E_{\alpha},F_{\alpha},\phi_{\alpha},\eta_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E,F,\phi,\eta)\to(E_{\alpha},F_{\alpha},\phi_{\alpha},\eta_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=V(\Phi(\eta))\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=V(\Phi(\eta))\subset\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:X\to S</annotation></semantics></math>. The Kosul resolution of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow(\gamma^{*}\det F)(-4)\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F(-2)\xrightarrow{\Phi(\eta)}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">η</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow(\gamma^{*}\det F)(-4)\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F(-2)\xrightarrow{\Phi(\eta)}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">and is exact since <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> has the right codimension at all <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>.
We break this into two sequences</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow(\gamma^{*}\det F)(-4)\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F(-2)\rightarrow A\rightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow(\gamma^{*}\det F)(-4)\rightarrow\gamma^{*}F(-2)\rightarrow A\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">and</span></p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow A\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow A\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">Pushing forward (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2. The category of quadruple covers ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>) we get a short exact sequence on <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{S}\rightarrow\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow R^{1}\gamma_{*}A\rightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{S}\rightarrow\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X}\rightarrow R^{1}\gamma_{*}A\rightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">Using (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2. The category of quadruple covers ‣ 3. The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ Tautological classes on Low-degree Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>), we obtain isomorphisms <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}\gamma_{*}A\cong R^{2}\gamma_{*}(\gamma^{*}\det F)(-4)\cong\det F\otimes R^{2}\gamma_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(-4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}\gamma_{*}A\cong R^{2}\gamma_{*}(\gamma^{*}\det F)(-4)\cong\det F\otimes R^{2}\gamma_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(-4)</annotation></semantics></math>. Because the dualizing sheaf of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega_{\gamma}=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(-3)\otimes\gamma^{*}\det E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\gamma}=\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(-3)\otimes\gamma^{*}\det E</annotation></semantics></math>, using Serre duality, we obtain an isomorphism <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}\gamma_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(-4)\cong\det E^{\vee}\otimes E^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}\gamma_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{P}E^{\vee}}(-4)\cong\det E^{\vee}\otimes E^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now the universal property of cokernel produces an isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}^{\vee}=\coker(\mathcal{O}_{S}\to\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X})\xrightarrow{\sim}R^{1}\gamma_{*}A\cong\det F\otimes\det E^{\vee}\otimes E^{\vee}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}^{\vee}=\coker(\mathcal{O}_{S}\to\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X})\xrightarrow{\sim}R^{1}\gamma_{*}A\cong\det F\otimes\det E^{\vee}\otimes E^{\vee}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.6.1" class="ltx_text">Meanwhile <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> determines an isomorphism <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det F\otimes\det E^{\vee}\cong\mathcal{O}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det F\otimes\det E^{\vee}\cong\mathcal{O}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Composing with this, and dualizing, we obtain an isomorphism <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi:E\to E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi:E\to E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Next, we have a commuting diagram</span></p><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="73.84" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-36.92px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 332.94 73.84" width="332.94"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,73.84) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(166.47,0) translate(0,36.92)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -166.47 -29.17)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 73.84)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.33)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(9.42,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces 0}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 22.78 0) translate(44.58,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -5.41 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="10.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces F}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 86.61 0) translate(62.13,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -22.97 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.32em;--ltx-fo-height:0.81em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.95" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.26)" width="45.93"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Sym^{2}E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Sym^{2}E}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 181.61 0) translate(66.25,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -27.09 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.92em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="54.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p6.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X}(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation en